{"videoId":"oieiYvQ9Rxo","title":"Is Void Linux Good? - With Jake from ‪@JakeLinux‬","author":"The Linux Cast [Matthew Weber]","authorId":"UCylGUf9BvQooEFjgdNudoQg","published":1749941093000,"description":"The boys are back! This time we&apos;re joined by Jake from  https://www.youtube.com/@JakeLinux. We&apos;ll be talking about Void and Window Managers.\n---\n\n```\n<b>*Contact Info*</b>\n\nSubscribe at http://thelinuxcast.org/\n\nPatreon https://patreon.com/thelinuxcast\n\nSubscribe on YouTube - https://youtube.com/thelinuxcast \n\nTyler on YouTube - https://youtube.com/ZaneyOG\n\nDrew on YouTube - https://youtube.com/JustAGuyLinux\n\nNate on YouTube - https://youtube.com/NatePicksTechWorld\n\nJake on YouTube - https://www.youtube.com/channel/UC1yGcBvdPGxRIMT1yo_bKIQ\n\nJake&apos;s website: jpedmedia.com\n\nJake&apos;s Email: jake@jpedmeida.com\n\nJake&apos;s merch: https://jakelinux.myspreadshop.com/\n\nEmail - email@thelinuxcast.org\n\nContact Info at https://thelinuxcast.org/contact\n\n[show notes]\nhttps://git.thelinuxcast.org/notes/s09/919.md\n\n[Time stamps]\n00:00:00 Intro\n00:00:41 Mini Topic: Our Favorite Bash Scripts\n00:08:55 Into The Void\n00:28:11 Window Manager Nerds Talk Window Managers\n00:53:23 Nuggies of the Week\n00:53:46 Jake&apos;s Nuggie\n00:54:59 Drew&apos;s Nuggie\n00:56:19 Matt&apos;s Nuggie\n00:58:21 Contact Info and Goodbyes","viewCount":24143,"lengthSeconds":3744,"watchProgress":1214.546988,"timeWatched":1791583909220,"isLive":false,"type":"video","_id":"fj6MNXhxiGGDzpVc","lastViewedPlaylistType":"","lastViewedPlaylistItemId":null}
{"videoId":"CMyflGZPZFE","title":"The Great DESOLATION of Oklahoma","author":"American Hobo - Train Riding","authorId":"UCaXUDj54dIDRtPUEcXNuObA","published":1790460006000,"description":"‼️ DISCLAIMER ‼️\n\nEntertainment only! Do NOT attempt to duplicate what you see in this video. This activity could result in serious bodily harm or death. Do not try and recreate any of the scenes. A.H. does not encourage or endorse freight hopping. This video is an act of artistic expression. A.H. is not responsible for other people&apos;s action. Trespassing is illegal.\n\n•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\n❤️ PLEASE DO ME A FAVOR SO THAT THIS CHANNEL GETS RECOGNIZED BY THE ALGORITHM...\n\n1) SUBSCRIBE \n\n2) LIKE EVERY VIDEO EVEN IF YOU DON&apos;T \n\n3) SHARE MY VIDEOS WITH AS MANY PEOPLE THAT YOU CAN EVEN IF YOU DON&apos;T LIKE THE PERSON.\n\nTHIS ALLOWS ME TO KEEP ON MAKING VIDEOS. \n\n\n•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\n\n🔴 My Other Channel on YouTube - American Hobo 🔴\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/channel/UCAQ3zSCMTvKBqPtNLu_fjSA\n\n•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\n\n🟢 American Hobo on Rumble 🟢 \n\nhttps://rumble.com/c/c-7909332\n\n•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\n\n📌 Telegram Group;\nhttps://t.me/americanhobo2026\n\n•••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\n\n💲 Donate 💲 Donate 💲 Donate 💲\n\n*PayPal \nhttps://www.paypal.com/donate/?business=2LS6KXBW39YBA&no_recurring=0&item_name=An+American+Hobo+traveling+the+world.&currency_code=USD\n\n*Cash App\nhttps://cash.app/$AmericanHobo123\n\n*Buy Me a Cup of Coffee \nhttps://www.buymeacoffee.com/kingmax738qt\n\n*Patreon\nhttps://www.patreon.com/AmericanHobo","viewCount":152934,"lengthSeconds":2055,"watchProgress":21.478745,"timeWatched":1792183897501,"isLive":false,"type":"video","_id":"foA2ooKDZi1w5w0b","lastViewedPlaylistType":"","lastViewedPlaylistItemId":null}
{"videoId":"EXJitIB-kaI","title":"Rick Bayless Chocoflan: The Impossible Cake","author":"Rick Bayless","authorId":"UC7GcvD-x1gnb8zfsXR5Chxg","published":1657732500000,"description":"Recipe 👉 https://www.rickbayless.com/recipe/chocoflan-aka-pastel-imposible-or-impossible-cake/\n\nThe first time I tasted this was a couple of decades ago in a market stall in Merida.  It blew me away:  chocolate cake on the bottom fused with a rich, creamy flan on the top.  I quickly went on the hunt for a recipe, which was pretty easy to find, given that most recipes are based on prepared ingredients (chocolate cake mix, evaporated milk, sweetened condensed milk) and easy for non-bakers to execute.  Recipes abound, but history does not.  Given the prepared ingredients, I guessed it was first concocted in the test kitchen of a prepared foods company.  But to date, I have not found one claiming it.  Nor anyone else, which seems odd for such a crazy-good, crazy-fun-to-make cake.\n\nHere’s what you do:  you smear caramely cajeta over a deep pan, spread in chocolate cake batter, then carefully top it with a liquidy custard. In the oven, the cake’s leavening is activated, filling the batter with bubbles causing it to rise through the flan (it does this in clumps) and reform into a chocolate cake layer.  The cake batter that started out below the flan finishes on top as a beautifully cooked cake layer.  You turn the whole thing out onto a platter and there it sits: chocolate cake topped with flan, the cajeta standing in for the flans typical caramelized sugar crown. \n\nThough the boxed-cake-mix recipes abound, I wanted to make chocoflan from scratch, so I contacted a professional baker friend in Puebla and asked if she had one.  Hers is the recipe I’ve used for years, though it calls for a 10-inch cake pan that’s 3 inches deep, not a pan the average cook owns.  Here I’ve scaled her recipe to fit in a Bundt pan (the internet tells me this is the most common way to bake chocoflan), which actually makes the baking quicker and more uniform.  \n\nI’ve fiddled with the cake, too, nudging it closer to a devil’s food cake and more streamlined than my original.  Some recipes call for cream cheese in the flan (making it what’s know as a flan—or queso—napolitano).  My version is a little lighter, which I love. \n\nThough this recipe may appear daunting, I will relay that my mother, in her eighties, decided this was her dessert and made it for many special occasions.  And she never considered herself much of a baker.","viewCount":123043,"lengthSeconds":900,"watchProgress":899.633921,"timeWatched":1792083451838,"isLive":false,"type":"video","_id":"h0mBv2FxlXIwgNth","lastViewedPlaylistType":"","lastViewedPlaylistItemId":null}
{"videoId":"nZIULawlb6w","title":"Day 469: stay stealthy [bicycle nomad]","author":"The Loaded Pickle","authorId":"UCXHO8wBQzaKwgnewkG4BmhQ","published":1791404113000,"description":"woke up to you know what...FOG. Packed up wet and got on the road. had some amazing riding thru the day. Stopped at a store listed as Ace Hardware, happened to be a grocery store as well. picked up some supplies and something good for dinner tonight. was treated awesome by TMI road crew then had one of my closest calls yet with a CalTrans plow truck. found the campground, no good, so I searched and searched for a stealth camp and finally found one. got settled in and made dinner, fog rolled back in so I called it for the day.","viewCount":11845,"lengthSeconds":2191,"watchProgress":1273.617834,"timeWatched":1791581720270,"isLive":false,"type":"video","_id":"hMonkRSbMDV00eS4","lastViewedPlaylistType":"","lastViewedPlaylistItemId":null}
{"videoId":"Ni3VUkCAmKE","title":"Ground-Penetrating Radar Discovered Something Beneath Serpent Mound That Changes Everything","author":"The Helix Report","authorId":"UCSIY635D06diBmhOWd-0c8A","published":1792000806000,"description":"Located in Adams County, Ohio, the Great Serpent Mound has fascinated archaeologists, historians, and researchers for centuries. Stretching over 1,300 feet along an ancient meteorite impact crater, this massive prehistoric earthwork was long thought to be a simple surface structure.\n\nHowever, recent Ground-Penetrating Radar (GPR) surveys and subsurface imaging have revealed startling new anomalies directly beneath the serpent&apos;s coils. From hidden subterranean structures and earlier construction layers to complex magnetic anomalies, these findings are completely changing what we know about the Adena and Fort Ancient cultures who built and restored this ancient wonder.\n\nIn this video, we dive deep into:\n\nThe newest high-tech GPR scanning data from Serpent Mound\n\nHidden sub-surface features and ancient structural layers\n\nHow the mound aligns with astronomical phenomena and the underlying impact crater\n\nWhat this means for the true timeline of ancient North America\n\n📌 Subscribe for more deep dives into archaeology, ancient mysteries, and forgotten history!","viewCount":49362,"lengthSeconds":1955,"watchProgress":252.17247,"timeWatched":1792090933518,"isLive":false,"type":"video","_id":"hbIh1M9MGAgTBvRf","lastViewedPlaylistType":"","lastViewedPlaylistItemId":null}
